    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Faulting along pre-existing discontinuities such as joint zones may be a common deformation process in crustal rocks. Faults formed in this manner will be influenced by the initial geometry of the joint zones. Documentation of the evolution of these types of faults is a prerequisite for formulating a comprehensive catalogue of faulting processes. In particular, cataclasis, secondary fracture formation and the arrangement of shear surfaces will be effected by the inherited fault zone structure, Fragmentation models of crustal shear zones predict characteristic grain size distributions. Relating a\r\nmode of formation of faults (for example, faults initiated from pre-existing joint zones) to a particular petrophysical properties such as  grain size distribution enables characterization of porous media flow properties based on either prior knowledge of the fault style, or physical evidence of grain size distribution from the subsurface.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
